<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Merchandising.Api - authentication, authorization, business rules,
    database access, transactions, reporting, audit, and API health.
    Spec section 7.

    ====================================================================
    THIS IS A PLAIN SHELL. IT IS NOT THE WEB API YET. THAT IS P1-02.
    ====================================================================

    At P1-01 this project is deliberately Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk" with the
    default library output. It has its project references wired and it
    compiles. That is the whole of P1-01's scope for it.

    P1-02 converts it to Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web" with OutputType=Exe,
    adds Program.vb (Module Program / Sub Main - NOT top-level statements,
    which do not exist in Visual Basic), and adds HealthController.

    Do not do any of that early. P1-02 is the rung A proof that resolves
    ADR-001 and retires this project's dominant risk: there is no Visual
    Basic ASP.NET Core template, so the Web SDK conversion has to be
    observed happening, under P1-02's own acceptance checks, and captured
    in evidence/phase-1/p1-02-project-file.txt. A conversion that has
    already quietly happened here leaves ADR-001 resolved by inference
    instead of by evidence.

    Note for whoever runs P1-01: this project's NAME is what activates the
    MerchGuardrails target in Directory.Build.targets - not its SDK. So the
    guardrail script runs on P1-01's build, not P1-02's.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Merchandising.Api</RootNamespace>
    <TargetFramework>$(MerchNetTfm)</TargetFramework>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Merchandising.Domain\Merchandising.Domain.vbproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Merchandising.Contracts\Merchandising.Contracts.vbproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Merchandising.Infrastructure\Merchandising.Infrastructure.vbproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
